>> title: Apple and Pear Pork Bone Soup
>> category: Soups
>> tags: pork, apple, pear, apricot kernels, cantonese, old-fire soup, autumn, stovetop
>> counters: The Soup Pot
>> aka: 蘋果雪梨南北杏豬骨湯, ping gwo syut lei naam bak hang zyu gwat tong, ping gwo suet lei nam bak hang jue gwat tong, apple and pear pork bone soup, apple snow pear and apricot kernel soup, 老火湯, lo fo tong
>> servings: 6
>> time: 3 hr
>> slack: forgiving — this pot goes wrong at the chopping board: fruit cut small dissolves into a cloudy sweetness the bones cannot cut
>> step.1: Autumn — 潤燥, the dry season. Fruit in a savoury pot is the thing about this genre most likely to surprise somebody meeting it for the first time. It is not there to make the soup sweet: three hours in, the apple and the pear have gone soft and given up their edges, and what comes out is savoury with a rounded back to it.
>> step.2: blanch from cold, then rinse the bones
>> step.3: rinse the dried goods
>> step.4: simmer 2 1/2 hr, barely a quiver
>> step.5: season at the end, never at the start
>> step.6: The broth is the dish. The solids — 湯渣, tong zaa — are spent by the end; they go on a side plate with soy sauce, or they do not.

Autumn — 潤燥, the dry season. Fruit in a savoury pot is the thing about this genre most likely to surprise somebody meeting it for the first time. It is not there to make the soup sweet: three hours in, the apple and the pear have gone soft and given up their edges, and what comes out is savoury with a rounded back to it.

Blanch @pork neck bones{1 1/2%lb}(680 g) from cold in @water{2%qt}(1.9 L) in a #soup pot{}, ~blanch{10%min} up to a rolling boil, then pour the whole lot away, rinse every bone under the tap and scrub the pot out.

Rinse @apricot kernels{1/4%cup}(naam bak hang; sweet and bitter together, about three to one — the bitter kind goes in small and always cooked through, never raw) and @honey dates{2}(mat zou; 潤, moistening — the same word the whole autumn family of pots turns on) under cold water.

Simmer @&(~2)blanched bones{} with @&(~1)dried goods{}, @apples{2}(cored and quartered, skin on), @snow pears{2}(syut lei — cored and quartered, skin on) and @cold water{3%qt}(2.8 L; the pot is filled once and not topped up) in the rinsed #soup pot{}, lid ajar, ~simmer{2 1/2%hr} at a bare quiver, unstirred.

Season @&(~1)soup{} with @kosher salt{1%tsp}, at the end and never at the start.

The broth is the dish. The solids — 湯渣, tong zaa — are spent by the end; they go on a side plate with soy sauce, or they do not.
